{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.11.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"gpu","dataSources":[{"sourceId":2126919,"sourceType":"datasetVersion","datasetId":1274570},{"sourceId":13989708,"sourceType":"datasetVersion","datasetId":8916692},{"sourceId":14104598,"sourceType":"datasetVersion","datasetId":8983332}],"dockerImageVersionId":31193,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":true}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n<h1>Classifying birdcalls</h1>\n<h3>Deep Learning in Python</h3>\n<h5>by Martha Brenner, Helena Vermeulen and Jette Roggow</h5>\n<h5>Normal group</h5>\n<h5>18/12/25</h5>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 1. Introduction \nBirds can be important ecological indicators. Their presence and behaviour can offer useful information about the health and state of their habitat like environmental quality and pollution. Monitoring bird populations can therefore be provide valuable insights into biodiversity trends and ecological changes (Remsen, 1994). However, manually analyzing huge amounts of acoustic data collected in nature is a labour-intensive task (Robinson et al. 2018). Machine learning can automate the bird call identification which enables researchers to process data that would otherwise be unused.\n\nIn this notebook, we want to create a neural network that could classify birdcalls to the corresponding bird. Our focus will be on making spectrograms from the audio recordings and feeding them to a convolutional neural network (CNN). This automatization can assist researchers in monitoring the biodiversity more effectively and can be interesting to a lot of biologists and researchers.\n\nTo accomplish our objective we divide our approach into a few steps; In the first step we describe our dataset en its background. Next, we prepare the data by converting the audio into spectrograms. After that, we fit a few different convolutional neural network (CNN) to our data and fine-tuned the results. Next, we test the accuracy of our model in our training and validation data set. In the last step, we discuss generalizability, limitations, and potential improvements for future work.\n\n# 2. Methods\n\n## 2.1 Description\n\n**1. Where do the data come from?(Generalization population)**  \nThe data come from Xeno-canto, a citizen-science platform where bird enthusiasts upload recordings. The training data (~60k clips, 397 species) include individual bird recordings from North, Central, and South America, resampled to 32 kHz. Because these are user uploads, labels may be incomplete, noisy, or wrong. The test and validation data consist of soundscape recordings from four sites (Eastern/Western US, Costa Rica, Colombia). Only two sites (Costa Rica, Sapsucker Woods) appear in validation. Thus, models generalize mainly to American bird species recorded in semi-natural soundscapes, but not necessarily to other continents or to clean studio-quality recordings. The dataset is highly imbalanced, with some species under 25 recordings, limiting generalization for rare species.\n\n**2. Data import**  \nOur data comes from the BIRDCLEF2021 competition and can be downloaded from the competition page as a zip file organised in a consistent format for easy access. Each bird type has several short training audios. The audio files are accompanied by metadata, including primary and secondary labels, latitudes, longitudes and dates. This information is stored in a CSV file and is used to link each audio clip to its corresponding species and contextual data.\n\n**3. Candidate machine learning methods (models + features)**  \nTypical approaches in BirdCLEF include:\n\n**Models:**\n* Deep CNNs (most common)\n* EfficientNet,\n* DenseNet,\n* ResNet (pretrained on ImageNet)\n* ResNeSt (split-attention ResNet variant)\n* Vision Transformers adapted to spectrograms\n* Ensembles of multiple architectures\n\n**Features:**\n* Mel-spectrograms as main input representation\n* Data augmentation: mixup, SpecAugment\n* Metadata features: time, date, location\n* Often used as post-filters to adjust predictions based on species occurrence likelihood\n\n**4. What is the Bayes’ error bound?**  \nThe Bayes error is the irreducible error of a classification task — the lowest possible error any model can achieve, even with infinite data and a perfect classifier. It reflects inherent ambiguity/noise in the data, such as:\n\n- overlapping classes\n- mislabeled samples\n- incomplete labels (common in BirdCLEF)\n- background noise masking calls In BirdCLEF 2021, the performance range gives an informal sense of this limit:\nBaseline (all \"nocall\") F1: 0.48\n- Best system F1: 0.69\n  \nIn the Kaggle Competition this data set origniates from, top submissions clustered closely (within ~2%) → suggests a performance plateau tied to dataset noise (Kahl et al. 2021). Because labels are often incomplete or wrong, the Bayes error is high in this competition, no system can perfectly classify segments when the ground truth itself is uncertain. Other studies using cleaner datasets report accuracies up to 97%, highlighting that BirdCLEF’s Bayes error is elevated due to label noise and complex soundscapes, not model limitations (Ansar et al., 2024).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Standard library\nimport os\nimport time\n\n# Scientific computing\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom scipy.signal import butter, sosfilt\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\n# Audio processing\nimport librosa\nimport librosa.display\n\n# Visualization\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Scikit-learn\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GroupShuffleSplit\n\n# TensorFlow / Keras\nimport tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow import keras\nfrom tensorflow.keras import layers, models\nfrom tensorflow.keras.callbacks import EarlyStopping, ReduceLROnPlateau\nfrom tensorflow.keras.applications import EfficientNetB0, ResNet50V2\nfrom tensorflow.keras.applications.efficientnet import preprocess_input as eff_preprocess\nfrom tensorflow.keras.applications.resnet_v2 import preprocess_input as resnet_preprocess","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T09:28:46.298101Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-12-21T09:28:46.298406Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T09:28:46.303845Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T09:28:46.298384Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T09:28:46.303084Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":26},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 2.2 Converting the audio data to spectograms.\n\n*In the end this required a lot of computing power and time. While this pipeline works and is funtional, we decided not to apply it, but instead to move forward with a preprocessed dataset, which already has this part done for us. Like this we still learned and understood how to perform this step, but saved time for refining our models.*\n\n\nTo prepare the data we first converted the audio of the bird calls into spectrograms. Those are images that represent aspects of the audio file. The x-axis of the image represents time, the y-axis represents different frequencies, and the pixel intensities represent the loudness of the different frequencies at the respective time points. The code from this step is greatly inspired/informed by https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/nlp/audio-classification-using-spectrograms/.\n\n\n## 2.2.1 Determining the length of audio to be converted \n\nBefore converting the audio to images, we needed to decide the time span of the window that we would divide the audio files into. When reviewing the literature, we noticed that most other researchers who attempted this endeavour used 3 to 5 second time intervals (Ghani et al., 2025; Xie & Zhu, 2022). Only few gave a reasoning for their decision of using their exact time span, though the widespread use of that time interval was an argument in itself. Based on our own argumentation we concluded that choosing a time span that was too short would mean that the birds couldn’t be classified accurately, but increasing the chosen time span would increase the computing power and time needed for our CNN to run. In the end, we created spectrograms from 3 second and 5 second windows and compared the performance of our CNN when using them. We also created overlap between the windows to make sure we don’t miss sounds that fall between windows. Another benefit of overlapping the windows is that it increases the data set. That could be helpful given the noise we can expect in the recordings.\n\n## 2.2.2 Data cleaning\n\nAfter that we augmented the data by removing the background noise. Lower frequencies are assoicated with wind and street noise more than bird noises (Madhusudhana et al., 2022), so we decided to remove some of them.  \n\n## 2.2.3 Creating the different spectrograms\n\nAfter that we created both MEL spectrograms and Short Time Fourier Transform (STFT) spectrograms to see how they compare. An STFT spectrogram represents sound as a time–frequency map by applying a Fourier transform to short, overlapping audio windows on a linear frequency scale, preserving detailed frequency information. A Mel spectrogram transforms this representation using the Mel scale, which reflects human pitch perception, compressing high-frequency information and emphasizing lower and mid-range frequencies to better align with perceptual relevance. Unfortunately, the notebooks tended to crash when converting the data to STFT spectrograms, which is why we chose MEL spectrograms for this task.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# # Setting the parameters\n# root_folder = \"/kaggle/input/bird-audio/train_short_audio\"\n# window_sec = 5                        # use 3 or 5 seconds\n# overlap = 0.25                        # 50% overlap\n# highpass_freq = 150                   # remove <150 Hz\n# sr = 22050                            # resample audio\n# n_mels = 128                          # Mel bins\n# \n# # Prepare filter for low frequencies\n# sos = butter(4, highpass_freq, btype='highpass', fs=sr, output='sos')\n# \n# X = []  # spectrograms\n# y = []  # labels\n# \n# max_folders = 5 # Load only first 5 folders\n# folder_count = 0","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.754151Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.754404Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.757954Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.754379Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.757194Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# for label in os.listdir(root_folder):\n#   label_path = os.path.join(root_folder, label)\n#   if not os.path.isdir(label_path):\n#     continue\n#   \n#   folder_count += 1\n#   if folder_count > max_folders:\n#     break\n#   \n#   print(f\"Processing folder: {label}\")\n#   \n#   # Loop through audio files\n#   for file_name in os.listdir(label_path):\n#     if not file_name.lower().endswith(('.wav', '.mp3', '.flac', '.ogg')):\n#     continue\n#   \n#   file_path = os.path.join(label_path, file_name)\n#   \n#   # Load audio\n#   audio, _ = librosa.load(file_path, sr=sr)\n#   \n#   # Skip very short audio\n#   window_samples = int(window_sec * sr)\n#   if len(audio) < window_samples:\n#     continue\n#   \n#   # High-pass filter\n#   audio = sosfilt(sos, audio)\n#   \n#   # Overlapping windows\n#   hop_samples = int(window_samples * (1 - overlap))\n#   \n#   for start in range(0, len(audio) - window_samples + 1, hop_samples):\n#     segment = audio[start:start + window_samples]\n#   \n#   # Mel spectrogram\n#   S = librosa.feature.melspectrogram(\n#     y=segment, sr=sr, n_mels=n_mels, fmax=sr//2\n#   )\n#   S_db = librosa.power_to_db(S, ref=np.max)\n#   \n#   X.append(S_db)\n#   y.append(label)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.758649Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.758884Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.770856Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.758862Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.770221Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# One spectrogram visualized\n#plt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\n#librosa.display.specshow(X[0], sr=sr)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.771410Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.771723Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.789950Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.771698Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T08:55:13.788976Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 2.3 Data Preparation using the Kkiller Dataset\n\nThis dataset computed 128-bin mel-spectrograms from audio resampled to 32 kHz, using 7-second chunks. Mels are extracted over 0–16 kHz with an FFT window of 3200 samples (appr. 100 ms) and a hop length of 800 samples (appr. 25 ms), resulting in 281 time frames per chunk. Consecutive chunks overlap via a step of about 4.66 s. With that the length of the chunks is even longer than what we originally planned to use, which will hopefully result in even higher quality outcomes.\n\n## 2.3.1 Loading in the meta-data and some filtering\n\n\nFrom the metadata we could get additional information from the data. For example there sometimes was a secondary label, if more than one bird was hearable in the audio. In one version we excluded all those recordings, as we hoped to get a clearer pattern then, which would make it easier for the network to classify the birds, but that did not improve accuracy. One reason for that is, that a lot of data was excluded due to that and the lower amount of data did just hurt the pattern learning and accuracy just as much.\n\nFurthermore in one version we excluded recordings which were rated with a low quality of below 3. However also that did not improve accuracy, which is why it is also commented out in this final version.\n\nNow we first load in the meta-data description.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# for checks\ndef show(msg, df=None, n=3):\n    print(f\"\\n✅ {msg}\")\n    if df is not None:\n        print(\"shape:\", df.shape)\n        display(df.head(n))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T09:28:55.320598Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-12-21T09:28:55.321145Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T09:28:55.325195Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T09:28:55.321126Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T09:28:55.324398Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":27},{"cell_type":"code","source":"META_PATH = \"/kaggle/input/kkiller-birdclef-2021/rich_train_metadata.csv\"\nmeta = pd.read_csv(META_PATH)\n\nmeta[\"secondary_labels\"] = meta[\"secondary_labels\"].astype(str).str.strip()\n\n# filtering\n# meta_clean = meta[(meta[\"secondary_labels\"] == \"[]\") & (meta[\"rating\"] >= 1.0)].copy()\n\n# no filtering\nmeta_clean = meta.copy()\n\nmeta_clean[\"recording_id\"] = meta_clean[\"filename\"].str.replace(\".ogg\", \"\", regex=False)\n\nshow(\"Filtered metadata\", meta_clean[[\"primary_label\",\"filename\",\"recording_id\",\"rating\",\"secondary_labels\"]])\n\n#checks\nassert len(meta_clean) > 0\nassert meta_clean[\"recording_id\"].notna().all()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T09:28:59.388476Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-12-21T09:28:59.389292Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T09:28:59.799054Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T09:28:59.389266Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T09:28:59.798418Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\n✅ Filtered metadata\nshape: (62874, 5)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"  primary_label      filename recording_id  rating secondary_labels\n0        acafly  XC109605.ogg     XC109605     2.5       ['amegfi']\n1        acafly   XC11209.ogg      XC11209     3.0               []\n2        acafly  XC127032.ogg     XC127032     3.0               []","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>primary_label</th>\n      <th>filename</th>\n      <th>recording_id</th>\n      <th>rating</th>\n      <th>secondary_labels</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>acafly</td>\n      <td>XC109605.ogg</td>\n      <td>XC109605</td>\n      <td>2.5</td>\n      <td>['amegfi']</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>acafly</td>\n      <td>XC11209.ogg</td>\n      <td>XC11209</td>\n      <td>3.0</td>\n      <td>[]</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>acafly</td>\n      <td>XC127032.ogg</td>\n      <td>XC127032</td>\n      <td>3.0</td>\n      <td>[]</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":28},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2.3.2 Loading the images\n\nEvery spectrogram from the kkiller-birdclef-2021 dataset is loaded. A new dataframe is added with the species label, filepath, segment index (most audio's have more than one spectrum) and recording id. The files itself were too large to load in one dataframe, so we only store the file paths and segments indices.  \n\nAfter this we combine the metadata with the spectra dataframe and make sure everything aligns. Lastly, we exclude birds with less than 20 samples here, as this is most likelly not enough to train that sound and will just confuse the network and we take a subset of the birds that have more than enough data to not make them too dominant. ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"IMAGE_ROOT = Path(\"/kaggle/input/kkiller-birdclef-2021/audio_images\")\n\nimage_records = []\nbad_shapes = 0\n\nfor species_dir in IMAGE_ROOT.iterdir():\n    if not species_dir.is_dir():\n        continue\n\n    species = species_dir.name\n\n    for npy_path in species_dir.glob(\"*.npy\"):\n        arr = np.load(npy_path, mmap_mode=\"r\")\n\n        if arr.ndim != 3:\n            bad_shapes += 1\n            continue\n\n        n_segments = arr.shape[0]  # e.g., 4\n\n        rec_id = npy_path.stem.replace(\".ogg\", \"\")  # \"XC....\"\n\n        for seg_idx in range(n_segments):\n            image_records.append({\n                \"primary_label\": species,\n                \"filepath\": str(npy_path),\n                \"segment_idx\": seg_idx,\n                \"recording_id\": rec_id\n            })\n\nimages_df = pd.DataFrame(image_records)\n\nshow(\"Indexed segment rows (raw)\", images_df)\n\nprint(\"Bad shape files skipped:\", bad_shapes)\nprint(\"Segments per file (example):\")\nprint(images_df.groupby(\"filepath\").size().head())\n\nassert len(images_df) > 0\nassert set([\"primary_label\",\"filepath\",\"segment_idx\",\"recording_id\"]).issubset(images_df.columns)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T09:29:02.922602Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-12-21T09:29:02.923245Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T09:35:28.077161Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T09:29:02.923223Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T09:35:28.076473Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\n✅ Indexed segment rows (raw)\nshape: (463253, 4)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"  primary_label                                           filepath  \\\n0        caltow  /kag