true).mean()\n\n\ntop5_cnn = top_k_accuracy_score(\n    y_true, p_cnn, k=5, labels=np.arange(p_cnn.shape[1])\n)\n\n#effnet\np_effnet = model_effnet.predict(test_ds_224, verbose=0)\ny_pred_effnet = np.argmax(p_effnet, axis=1)\n\nacc_effnet = (y_pred_effnet == y_true).mean()\n\ntop5_effnet = top_k_accuracy_score(\n    y_true, p_effnet, k=5, labels=np.arange(p_effnet.shape[1])\n)\n\n#resnet\np_resnet = model_resnet.predict(test_ds_224, verbose=0)\ny_pred_resnet = np.argmax(p_resnet, axis=1)\n\nacc_resnet = (y_pred_resnet == y_true).mean()\n\ntop5_resnet = top_k_accuracy_score(\n    y_true, p_resnet, k=5, labels=np.arange(p_resnet.shape[1])\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T13:55:35.200561Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T13:55:35.200767Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-12-21T13:55:35.200669Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-12-21T13:55:35.200678Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 3. Results","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# bar chart\n\nresults_df = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"model\": [\"CNN\", \"EfficientNet\", \"ResNet\"],\n    \"accuracy\": [acc_cnn, acc_effnet, acc_resnet],\n    \"top5_accuracy\": [top5_cnn, top5_effnet, top5_resnet],\n})\n\nresults_df\n\n\nx = np.arange(len(results_df))\nwidth = 0.4\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(14, 5))\nplt.bar(x - width/2, results_df[\"accuracy\"], width, label=\"Accuracy\")\nplt.bar(x + width/2, results_df[\"top5_accuracy\"], width, label=\"Top-5 Accuracy\")\n\nplt.xticks(x, results_df[\"model\"], rotation=90)\nplt.ylabel(\"Accuracy\")\nplt.title(\"Accuracy vs Top-5 Accuracy across the Models\")\nplt.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-12-21T13:55:35.185472Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-12-21T13:55:35.185770Z","execution_failed":"2025-12-21T14:35:16.422Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"![BarChart.png](attachment:c57154ed-b096-4810-afdd-566dd3a239e4.png)","metadata":{},"attachments":{"c57154ed-b096-4810-afdd-566dd3a239e4.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"All models were evaluated using the validation accuracy and the top-5 validation accuracy. ResNet50 performed best among all evaluated models with a validation accuracy around 10% and top-5 accuracy around 45%. It consistently achieved the highest validation accuracy. This strong performance can be attributed to pre-training on ImageNet and residual connections enabling robust feature extraction from spectrograms. Baseline CNN showed the weakest performance. This could be expected since it was trained from scratch and lacked pre-trained image features. EfficientNetB0 achieved moderate performance, balancing accuracy and computational efficiency. \n\nThe accuracy and loss plots indicated that our model mainly suffered from overfitting. Validation loss began to increase after a limited number of epochs while training loss stayed around 0, indicating poor generalization to unseen data. This suggests that stronger regularization techniques, such as dropout or data augmentation, could be beneficial. Additionally, earlier stopping or reducing model complexity may help mitigate this behavior.\n\nEnsemble approaches performed well in separate notebooks, indicating that combining multiple models can improve robustness and accuracy. Full ensemble evaluation was limited by long training times and computational constraints. ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 4. Discussion and conclusion\n\nIn this notebook we evaluated CNN, EfficientNetB0, and ResNet models on BirdCLEF 2021 data, confirming the value of transfer learning. Our best performance reached 10% accuracy and 45% top-5 accuracy. The accuracy is above chance, but below benchmarks. BirdCLEF is a high–Bayes-error dataset with noisy labels and overlapping calls, limiting achievable performance. So for example, the models may struggle with no-call samples that are labeled as bird species. When a segment contains no actual bird call, the model is forced to associate background noise with a bird class, leading it to learn noise instead of the actual bird call. This indicates limited robustness to non-informative acoustic inputs such as background noise and silence.\n\nOur model can be generalized mainly to North, Central, and South American bird species recorded in semi-natural soundscapes similar to those in the training and validation data. However further generalization is limited, since only 25% of the available dataset was used, reducing data diversity. \n\nAnother limitation in our research was that the no-call data was not handled effectively. This limited the model’s ability to distinguish background noise from bird calls. Additionally, overall accuracy remained well below benchmarks and long training times constrained experimentation and hyperparameter tuning. \n\nFuture work for practitioners could consist of better labeling strategies for no-call spectrograms. It would also be interesting to explore ensemble methods by combining multiple models to improve performance and using computationally efficient techniques (e.g. model pruning, mixed precision, smaller architectures) to reduce training time and enable experimentation.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 5. References\n\nAnsar, W., Chatterjee, A., Goswami, S., & Chakrabarti, A. (2024). An EfficientNet-Based Ensemble for Bird-Call Recognition with Enhanced Noise Reduction. SN Computer Science, 5(2). https://doi.org/10.1007/s42979-023-02591-6\n\nAudio classification using spectrograms. (2025, July 23). GeeksforGeeks. https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/nlp/audio-classification-using-spectrograms/\n\nGhani, B., Kalkman, V.J., Planqué, B. et al. Impact of transfer learning methods and dataset characteristics on generalization in birdsong classification. Sci Rep 15, 16273 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-00996-2\n\nKahl, S., Denton, T., Klinck, H., Glotin, H., Goëau, H., Vellinga, W., Planqué, R., & Joly, A. (2021). Overview of BirdCLEF 2021: Bird call identification in soundscape recordings. Agritrop (Cirad), 1437–1450. http://agritrop.cirad.fr/612697/\n\nMadhusudhana, S., Pavan, G., Miller, L. A., Gannon, W. L., Hawkins, A., Erbe, C., ... & Thomas, J. A. (2022). Choosing equipment for animal bioacoustic research. Exploring Animal Behavior Through Sound: Volume, 37. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-97540-1_2\n\nRemsen, J. V. (1994). Use and Misuse of Bird Lists in Community Ecology and Conservation. The Auk, 111(1), 225–227. https://doi.org/10.2307/4088531\n\nRobinson, W. D., Lees, A. C., & Blake, J. G. (2018). Surveying tropical birds is much harder than you think: a primer of best practices. Biotropica, 50(6), 846–849. Portico. https://doi.org/10.1111/btp.12608\n\nXie, J., & Zhu, M. (2022). Sliding-window based scale-frequency map for bird sound classification using 2D-and 3D-CNN. Expert Systems with Applications, 207, 118054. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2022.118054","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 6. Division of labour\n\n**Martha:** Built the spectograms, made the poster, did some fine-tuning on the models and focussed on the pitch. <br>\n**Helena:** Fitted the CNN and Efficientnet (including the preprocessing), improved the markdown and other written text on the notebook <br>\n**Jette:** Fitted the resnet and made the ensemble (including the preprocessing), made the function for loading the data step by step and added markdown. ","metadata":{}}]}